Cassette-Spill

Ingredients: INDIE FOLK & FREAKFOLK + ELECTROPOP + TRIPPY, SURREAL & PSYCHEDELIC + Bongos
Creative Catalyst: Reverse-engineered from a corrupted cassette found in a time capsule.

Cassette-Spill is the sound of hyper-digital pop melodies melting through rotted magnetic tape, anchored by intimate, untamed acoustic picking. It takes the beat-driven, sparkling polish of electropop and deliberately ruins it: pitched-down vocal chops act like acoustic drone strings, while raw freakfolk fingerpicking is shattered into rhythmic, sidechained synth-lines. Instead of acting as a breezy Latin-jazz accompaniment, real skin-and-wood bongos are played with metal thimbles and processed through sub-bass pitch-shifters, creating a surreal, wet, thumping heartbeat that feels both prehistoric and synthetic. 

Genre DNA
* Tempo & Pulse: 78–112 BPM. A slurred, drunken swing where precision 808 hi-hats stutter against loose, hand-slapped bongo polyrhythms.
* Harmonic Palette: Lydian dominant modes colliding with warped, detuned open-G guitar tunings. Bright, sugary electropop chord progressions that suddenly decay into microtonal folk drones.
* Production Signatures: 
    1. Spectral Magnet-Baking: Vocal takes and synth arpeggios are printed onto physical 1980s tape, heated with a heat gun to physically blister the iron oxide layer, and digitized back into the DAW to create random, irreversible harmonic dropouts.
    2. Thimble-Slapped Sub-Bongos: High-pitched bongo heads are hit with stainless steel thimbles and routed through an envelope follower that triggers an sub-octave sine wave, turning acoustic hand percussion into an earth-shattering bass drum.
    3. Granular Banjo Glitch: Freakfolk clawhammer banjo patterns are fed into a real-time granular sampler, freezing acoustic notes into glittering, glassy electropop pads that hover behind the vocal.
* Dynamics: Extreme, unpredictable shifts. A song begins as a whispered, dry acoustic ballad recorded on a single room mic before violently exploding into a staggering, neon-soaked psychedelic drop with heavy synth-bass and smeared vocal loops.

Genre Scene & Origin Lore
Born in late 2026 within the damp, forgotten sub-basements of Leeds, Cassette-Spill emerged after art student and freakfolk archivist Silas Thorne unburied a 1994 municipal time capsule containing a severely water-damaged, magnetic-melted mixtape. Attempting to restore the audio, Thorne ran the warbled, iron-oxide sludge through modern AI demixing stem-splitters; the software panicked, inventing strange, neon-hued synth glitches where folk guitars used to be and interpreting static hiss as hyper-quantized electropop percussion. Local buskers and underground rave producers quickly formed a collective called "The Oxide-Eaters." Scene fashion rejects sleek clubwear in favor of thrifted, hand-embroidered tweed jackets covered in unspooled, iridescent brown magnetic tape strands, safety-pinned neon glowsticks, and heavy copper thimbles worn on every finger. Rituals involve "Tape-Baking Parties" at midnight, where audience members throw old cassette tapes into iron cauldrons filled with warm salt water to create localized, corrupt audio artifacts played live over low-frequency subwoofers while dancing to erratic bongo circles.

Instruments
* Central & Signature: Micro-tuned skin-and-wood Bongos (played with thimbles), Melted-Tape Granular Synthesizers, 12-string Acoustic Guitars with copper pick-ups.
* Supporting: Analog Bass Synths (Minimoog), circuit-bent Casio keyboards, open-back banjos, electro-pop Vocoders, vintage Mellotron string patches.

For Fans Of: Animal Collective, CocoRosie, Sylvan Esso, The Books

